    "issued_at": "2026-11-13T08:29:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "4065e233-01ec-4f17-aad1-dd0410c2668a",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 130829\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n129 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- An incoming weather system will begin to cool temperatures and\n  increase rain chances today and Saturday.\n\n- Sunday and Monday will be warm and dry before a series of \n  weather disturbances will bring occasional rain chances and \n  cooling temperatures back to the Desert Southwest by Tuesday.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nThe short wave trough that will bring cooler and wetter weather to \nthe Desert SW starting today is currently in the Eastern Pacific \njust off the southern CA coastline. This system will move quickly, \npassing through southern AZ and Northern Mexico over the course of \ntoday. PWATs will increase to near 200% of normal, which, in \nconjunction with the energy from the short wave troughing feature \nrain chances will also increase, with PoPs peaking (30-60%) this \nafternoon across south-central AZ, then into the eastern higher \nterrain heading into the evening hours. Most of the expected shower \nactivity will remain relatively light, talking about a few hundredths \nto at most a tenth of an inch, a few isolated thunderstorms \nresulting in briefly heavier rainfall rates cannot be ruled out \ngiven that there will be a least a few hundredths J/KG of CAPE \npresent. \n\nBehind the short wave just discussed is another short wave that will \nquickly move in from the northwest. However this next system will \nremain across northern AZ. This will lead to rain chances being \nmostly confined to the north with the lower deserts forecasted to be \ndry, while the higher terrain areas could squeeze out an additional \n0.05\"-0.10\" Saturday. \n\nOutside of rain chances, these systems will also being in cooler \ntemperatures as H5 heights will drop between 564-567 dam on Friday \nand 570-573 dam on Saturday. Afternoon highs will be in the upper \n70s for Friday then as the first short wave passes temperatures will \ncool into the low 70s across south-central AZ while out west \ntemperatures will remain in the mid to upper 70s. after the first \nsystem passes low temperatures will lag in response, resulting in \nmorning lows in the mid to upper 50s starting Saturday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nSunday will be a down day for shower activity with a brief period\nof shortwave ridging and some slight dry air advection from the \nnorthwest. This will lead to considerably less clouds, virtually \nno rain chances and temperatures climbing back into the upper 70s \nby Monday. Models remain in good agreement that another northern \nPacific waves will continue to be ejected southeastward toward the\nU.S. West Coast starting early next week. Currently models \nsuggest the next potential system reaching the California coast \nearly Monday before reaching our region Monday night into Tuesday,\nwhere additional rain chances will return to the forecast with \nNBM PoPs peaking between 50-80% in south-central AZ and the higher\nterrain areas, however this far out this may need to be adjusted \nbased of how the track of this low progresses. By Wednesday models\nare pointing towards yet again another low system, however larger\ndiscrepancies between models are evident for this system. Due to \nthis confidence is low for rain chances by mid to late next week \nas things are too inconsistent at this time. \n\nDespite if these next systems bring rain to the region or not, a \nmore significant cooling trend can be counted on by the middle of\nthe week as these series of weather systems move in from the \nnorthwest. They will likely usher in a cooler air mass with \nafternoon highs trending more towards the upper 60s to low 70s,\nthe higher or lower end of this range largely being dependent on\nthe depth and position of the systems. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0520Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nA period of VCSH/SHRA with gusty erratic winds and CIGs as low as\n6-8K ft AGL is anticipated Friday afternoon, mainly between\n20-02Z. An isolated storm or two may also develop, but chances are\ntoo low (10%) for inclusion in the 06Z TAF package. Light easterly\nwinds are favored through Friday morning. There is moderate\nconfidence winds will veer SSW-SSE by Friday afternoon before\nturning back E-SE by the evening. SCT to BKN mid and high clouds\nwill be common through most of the TAF period. Lower clouds, with\nbases around 4-6K ft AGL, are expected Friday night into Saturday\nmorning and additional low-topped showers are likely to develop\nSaturday morning, but mostly stay north and east of the terminals.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nWinds at KIPL will favor a W'rly component through most of the TAF\nperiod. KBLH will see VRB through much of tonight and Friday\nmorning before an increase in S'rly winds develops. Wind speeds at\nboth terminals will remain light through Friday morning and then\nincrease heading into the afternoon and evening with occasional\ngusts up to 20 kts at KBLH and 20-25 kts at KIPL. An increase in\nclouds Friday morning in expected, with lowest bases down to\naround 7K ft AGL. Cloud coverage decreases again through the\nafternoon and evening.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nA more unsettled weather pattern will begin to develop starting\ntoday and is expected to persist well into next week as a series \nof weather disturbances moving through the region will bring \noccasional rain chances and cooler temperatures. Minimum humidity \nvalues will increase today to between 30-40%. These higher minimum\nhumidity are expected to remain elevated through much of next \nweek. Expect overall light winds today before increasing Friday \nthrough early Saturday, mainly across the western districts, with \npeak gusts mostly staying under 25-30 mph.\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...\n\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area through at least early next week due to \nwater released from the dam. Water release levels have now risen\nto 3000 cfs and are expected to remain at this release level \nthrough the next several days. This will have the potential to \nflood farm fields and low-water crossings along the river \ndownstream from the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Ryan \nAVIATION...Benedict \nFIREWEATHER...Lojero/Ryan \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero\n",
